<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Privacy manifest for the App target.

	Foldwing itself has no server, no account and sends nothing anywhere, so
	NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is empty: everything the App Privacy label
	declares (Device ID, Product Interaction, both for third-party advertising
	and both used for tracking) is collected by Google AdMob, whose own
	manifests ship inside GoogleMobileAds and UserMessagingPlatform. Declaring
	it a second time here would claim the app collects data it never touches.

	NSPrivacyTracking is FALSE, and that is not a loophole — the key describes
	what THIS binary does with data, and this binary has no server, no
	analytics and makes no network call of its own. Asking for ATT permission
	is not itself tracking; the tracking is AdMob's, and AdMob answers for it
	in its own manifest. The App Privacy label on App Store Connect is where
	tracking is disclosed to users, and it still declares Device ID and
	Product Interaction as used for tracking.

	It was TRUE for builds 36-39, every one of which Apple returned as
	INVALID_BINARY within ninety seconds, because Apple requires at least one
	entry in NSPrivacyTrackingDomains whenever NSPrivacyTracking is true and
	this array was empty. The rule is enforced ONLY on App Store submission:
	all four builds processed to VALID for TestFlight, and altool's validate-app
	reported no errors on the same ipa. Build 12, the last one Apple accepted,
	predates this file entirely.

	(Keep double hyphens out of this comment. A plist is XML, and XML forbids
	them inside comments: plutil -lint still passes, but a strict parser does
	not, and there is no reason to hand Apple the question.)

	Do not "fix" it by listing Google's ad domains. iOS blocks traffic to a
	declared tracking domain once the user denies ATT, which would leave most
	of the audience with no ads at all. Google omits the key for that reason.

	The two accessed-API entries exist because the Capacitor plugins that use
	them ship NO manifest of their own (verified: @capacitor/preferences and
	@capacitor/filesystem contain no .xcprivacy; only @capacitor/ios core does),
	so the app target has to declare them or Apple returns ITMS-91053.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- @capacitor/preferences → UserDefaults. Progress is read and
			     written only by this app, for this app. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- @capacitor/filesystem → the share card is written into the app's
			     own Cache directory and handed to the share sheet. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
